<main class="wrap">
<div class="view" id="v-home">
<div class="hero">
<h1>It’s on the tip of my <button class="blank" id="blank" aria-live="polite" aria-label="Reveal the word. Press to try another language.">tongue</button><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"><span>.</span><span>.</span><span>.</span></span></h1>
<p class="lede">Short videos on how bilinguals remember, forget, and almost say it. Real memory research, one minute at a time, from grad students who study how we remember written and spoken bilingual discourse.</p>
<p class="hint">Tap the dashed word to say it in another language.</p>
</div>
<section>
<h2>Video highlights</h2>
<div class="grid" id="highlights"></div>
<p><a class="more" href="#videos">See all videos</a></p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>About</h2>
<p>We started Tip of my Tongue to share what the science says about bilingual memory, and to be honest about what grad school is like while we figure it out.</p>
<div class="host">
<div class="photo">Host photo</div>
<p>Hi, I’m <span class="fill">your name</span>, a <span class="fill">year</span> PhD student in <span class="fill">department</span> at <span class="fill">university</span>. I study <span class="fill">your research topic</span>. The language I lose words in most is <span class="fill">language</span>, and the last word stuck on the tip of my tongue was <span class="fill">word</span>.</p>
</div>
<div class="host">
<div class="photo">Co-host photo</div>
<p>And I’m <span class="fill">co-host name</span>, a <span class="fill">year</span> PhD student in <span class="fill">department</span> at <span class="fill">university</span>. I study <span class="fill">research topic</span>. My favorite word in my second language is <span class="fill">word</span>, and I always forget <span class="fill">something you forget</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</div>

<div class="view" id="v-series" hidden>
<h1 class="page-title">Series</h1>
<dl>
<dt>On the Tip</dt><dd>One word-retrieval puzzle per video: why a word you know vanishes, and what your two languages have to do with it.</dd>
<dt>Read vs. Heard</dt><dd>Do you remember a story better when you read it or hear it? And does the answer change with the language?</dd>
<dt>Grad School, Translated</dt><dd>Committee meetings, reviewer comments, and funding, explained plainly for anyone figuring out academia.</dd>
<dt>Inside the Lab</dt><dd>How a psycholinguistics study actually gets built, run, and rewritten.</dd>
</dl>
</div>

